WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.520
Speaker 1: We had the 4th of July weekend
in the United States, big holiday weekend,

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:13.580
lots of fireworks, which I can really do without
and cookouts and all sorts of different things.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:18.180
But we also had an unconscionable number of
shootings.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.160
And very often this doesn't get news.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:26.790
I will sometimes come to you and say, hey,
you know, we had 15 shootings over the weekend

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:27.980
and 70 people died.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.860
And individually, none of these made any headlines.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:39.150
And part of that fact that that is both evidence
that we are incredibly increasingly desensitized

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:42.760
to the fact that this is just a regular part
of living in the United States.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:46.690
And also because increasingly shootings don't
make headlines.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:53.140
It's less present in terms of advocating for
gun safety, regulation and laws, et cetera.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:54.210
Look at these numbers.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:01:00.080
There is a CNN report from last night which
says at least one hundred and fifty people

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:06.950
fatally shot in more than 400 shootings over
the Fourth of July weekend.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:08.920
These numbers are now out of date.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:14.040
And I actually went to the gun violence archive,
which you can find at Gun Violence Archive.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.970
Doug and I just started counting.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:20.750
And as you can see, pages and pages of July
5th shootings.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:23.740
And with any single one of these, you can
click on it and get more information.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:30.910
I mean, just randomly, you know, look at this
shooting in South Carolina, Denmark, South

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:36.281
Carolina, where one person was killed, multiple
shootings in Greensboro, North Carolina, with

00:01:36.281 --> 00:01:37.320
people injured.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.540
And you just scroll by and the second page,
it's all July 5th shootings.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.830
The third page is all July 5th shootings.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:48.480
The fourth page is all July 5th shootings.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.570
And you just keep going.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:52.939
Eventually you get to shootings on July 4th.

00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:58.130
But it's just pages and pages and every single
one of these could be a news story.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:00.689
We're still on July 5th.

00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:07.909
One hundred and ninety two people killed in
five hundred and sixty two shootings over

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:08.909
the weekend.

00:02:08.909 --> 00:02:17.099
Now, I'm just talking about Saturday, July
3rd, Sunday, July 4th, and Monday, July 5th.

00:02:17.099 --> 00:02:19.189
There's already been shootings this morning.

00:02:19.189 --> 00:02:22.730
There was one in Atlanta and a couple of others.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:27.609
If you go back further into Friday, there
were shootings on that day.

00:02:27.609 --> 00:02:29.300
Think about these numbers.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:36.049
Five hundred sixty two shootings on just July
three, four and five and one hundred and ninety

00:02:36.049 --> 00:02:38.499
two people killed.

00:02:38.499 --> 00:02:43.609
CNN reporting some of the details from from
some of these, at least.

00:02:43.609 --> 00:02:45.599
And again, remember, this was from last night.

00:02:45.599 --> 00:02:47.500
So these numbers have been updated.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:52.120
At least one hundred and fifty people were
killed by gun violence in more than 400 shootings

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:57.670
across the country during the Fourth of July
weekend as cities nationwide confront a surge.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:02.230
In violent crime, the data, which includes
the number of shooting incidents and gun violence

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:06.689
victims nationally over a 72 hour period from
Friday to Sunday is still evolving and will

00:03:06.689 --> 00:03:11.340
be updated in New York, where gun violence
has been rising to levels not seen in years.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.510
There were 26 victims from 21 one shootings.

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.689
And you can just go and look at whatever city
is interesting to you.

00:03:17.689 --> 00:03:21.290
In Chicago, 83 people shot, including 14 killed.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:25.790
Now, the article accurately points out that
gun violence and violence in general has been

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:27.620
up over the last year.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:31.260
This is something we've talked about before,
and it's really important not to get sucked

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.319
into the disinformation about this.

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:40.129
Republicans who were silent about mass shootings
during the Trump administration, except in

00:03:40.129 --> 00:03:45.540
very narrow cases where the identity of either
the shooter or the victim was advantageous

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:46.540
to them politically.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:51.230
Those people are now saying it's violence
everywhere under Joe Biden.

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:55.200
That's that's really all they care about making
a political point.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:01.439
My suggestion is to, number one, zoom out
a little bit and understand that over a 20

00:04:01.439 --> 00:04:05.809
year period, over a 30 year period, violent
crime in the U.S., generally speaking, is

00:04:05.809 --> 00:04:07.040
down.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.400
We have a very specific problem in the United
States.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.379
It's a unique problem globally when it comes
to gun violence.

00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:19.810
There is a preponderance of both firearm ownership
as well as availability of firearms in the

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:20.989
United States.

00:04:20.989 --> 00:04:24.090
That is not the case in just about any other
developed country.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:27.720
And in addition to that, there's a cultural
problem that we've talked about.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:33.340
I don't want to rehash that right now because
what is missing from I hesitate to even call

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:37.790
it an analysis, but what's missing from the
talking points from the right about violent

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:40.640
crime is up in Trump in Biden's America.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:42.450
A few things are missing from that.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:51.060
Number one, violent crime was up during 20-20
during the pandemic and Donald Trump was president

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:52.060
at the time.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:57.720
And then instead of blaming the president,
they were blaming so so-called Democrat mayors

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:59.150
in blue cities.

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:00.150
So No.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:04.800
One, it's very transparent that no matter
who's in power, they will find Democrats to

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:05.810
blame.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:09.770
And then the truth is that this is a far more
complicated thing than there is a Democrat

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:11.990
in the White House or a Republican in the
White House.

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:17.830
It's far more complicated than the mayor or
the governor is a Republican or a Democrat.

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:21.170
This relates to economic issues.

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:30.120
This relates to instability more broadly of
our economic system, exacerbated by the pandemic

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:33.900
and what is definitely a case shaped recovery,
where those that have done well have done

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:38.610
really well as we have started to recover
from the pandemic and others have been completely

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:39.610
left behind.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:47.470
So the point here is not to relitigate every
aspect of so-called violent Democrat cities

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:53.180
which we've debunked, or of the fact that
the crime increases actually started in 2020

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:55.950
when it wasn't Joe Biden that was president,
it was Donald Trump.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:06:02.920
It's that you as hopefully a savvy new consumer
news consumer when you hear these talking

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:03.920
points.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:10.901
Well, my hope is your reaction would not only
be is the talking point accurate or not, but

00:06:10.901 --> 00:06:16.710
a slight zoom out where you say, is this even
the right conversation to be having about

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:17.710
this issue?

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:18.860
Because often it's not.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:24.150
They want us to be having the conversations
that are advantageous to them and they leave

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:28.810
out completely in this case, for example,
the economic conversation, the violence started

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:31.370
going up under Trump in twenty twenty.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:35.330
It's continued going up under Joe Biden in
some cities in twenty twenty one.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:37.590
One was a Republican, one a Democrat.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:41.900
Let's argue about that is what they want when
in reality the conversation we should be having

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:43.370
is a different one.

